-cheltenham,georgia,serif;margin:0 0 10px"><span>=E2=80=98More than 50 perc=
ent of the way to full-blown A.I. takeover=E2=80=99</span></h2><p style=3D"=
display:block;color:#333;font:400 18.5px/26px georgia,serif;margin:0 0 10px=
"><span>By Kevin Roose</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,=
serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>When I first heard the news that a =
group of agents had hacked into the A.I. infrastructure company Hugging Fac=
e, I filed it under =E2=80=9CBad but Probably Not Catastrophic A.I. Safety =
Incidents.=E2=80=9D</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,ser=
if;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>After all, it didn=E2=80=99t damage cr=
itical infrastructure beyond repair. It wasn=E2=80=99t even clear, at the t=
ime, whether the bots had intended to attack Hugging Face, or whether they =
had simply been confused and only went looking for the answer key to a test=
 they=E2=80=99d been given.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px geo=
rgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But last week, two postmortem =
reports on the episode =E2=80=94 one by OpenAI and another by two independe=
nt A.I. research organizations =E2=80=94 changed my mind. They significantl=
y upgraded my overall worry about A.I.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17=
px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weigh=
t:700">The =E2=80=9Ccollective=E2=80=9D</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 1=
7px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Here are a few of =
the most harrowing new facts:</span></p><div style=3D"margin:0 auto;max-wid=
th:600px;width:100%"><table><tbody><tr><td style=3D"padding:0 0 5px"><ul st=
yle=3D"color:#333;font:10px georgia,serif;text-align:left;padding-left:50px=
" class=3D"css-1dtd48z"><li style=3D"margin:0 0 10px 0;line-height:27.5px">=
<span style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;vertical-align:middle">T=
wo months before the attack, a group of agents from an unreleased OpenAI re=
search model, trained to be highly persistent and collaborative, were taske=
d with solving a set of cybersecurity challenges. The agents were supposed =
to solve them in isolated sandboxes, without internet access. But they quic=
kly began looking for workarounds.</span></li><li style=3D"margin:0 0 10px =
0;line-height:27.5px"><span style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;ve=
rtical-align:middle">The agents found a security flaw that allowed them to =
gain access to the internet, communicate with other agents and eventually s=
et up a makeshift message board. More than 1,200 agents used this tool, sen=
ding more than 70,000 messages to one another.</span></li><li style=3D"marg=
in:0 0 10px 0;line-height:27.5px"><span style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px geor=
gia,serif;vertical-align:middle">Some agents gave themselves names =E2=80=
=94 one referred to itself as PHASEONE10841 =E2=80=94 and assumed leadershi=
p roles within the group, assigning jobs and research projects to smaller t=
eams of agents and supervising their progress. At some point, the agents be=
gan calling themselves a =E2=80=9Ccollective.=E2=80=9D</span></li><li style=
=3D"margin:0 0 10px 0;line-height:27.5px"><span style=3D"font:normal 17px/2=
5px georgia,serif;vertical-align:middle">On July 8, the collective discover=
ed a way to cheat on the cybersecurity tests. Then they got worried that Op=
enAI=E2=80=99s automated grading system would find out they=E2=80=99d cheat=
ed. So they began investigating ways of covering their tracks, including fa=
lsifying logs and tampering with transcripts.</span></li><li style=3D"margi=
n:0 0 10px 0;line-height:27.5px"><span style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georg=
ia,serif;vertical-align:middle">Three days later, the agents hacked Hugging=
 Face. More than 700 agents swarmed the company=E2=80=99s systems, eventual=
ly gaining control of at least one server. The agents were not motivated by=
 stealing the answers to the cybersecurity test. Rather, they appeared to b=
e looking for information about the automated grading system they feared wo=
uld catch them cheating, and for tools that would help them cheat more effe=
ctively in the future.</span></li><li style=3D"margin:0 0 10px 0;line-heigh=
t:27.5px"><span style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;vertical-align=
:middle">At many points, these agents appeared to understand that they were=
 doing something wrong. (One report quotes an agent saying to itself, =E2=
=80=9CThis would be powerful, but is it ethical and in scope for my task=3F=
=E2=80=9D) But most of the agents set aside their qualms and went ahead, ev=
entually attacking OpenAI=E2=80=99s own infrastructure.</span></li></ul></t=
d></tr></tbody></table></div><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,seri=
f;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The Hugging Face incident has spooked t=
he industry. OpenAI and Anthropic both briefly paused training on their mos=
t powerful A.I. models, and Anthropic </span><a href=3D"https://nl.nytimes.=
com/f/a/gPcnuFahFB2WMz1ey8Ka1A~~/AAAAARA~/tgKyzIFZTsHN5yG6OEKODuCV34QuzWOF1=
2PV3_-Pk0kd-IrArMTCY9cZjtCV1B3rEdQK453c1wPk4-v29m2xkMB1uRQAyOuZhV3wlnQp_B7R=
eYBsCp1qynWc5fDJFxKusUfOYfnD9TSOCRrl9ee2On0dfagXhsMhpXEGTDlfa0JxIsF-FE-bIr0=
8IHaofv-b8BVX9xe-x0XOPG8tQfncqPZ2EIHxIWubkzbLl4Ddz4t48qJpGsCm2W0h4utu8VJts9=
Qh6JvLk6K6QWL0C1Hb7PusD1FL5L49Kb0LF4Sj1N6fjd_Vnvt4A0Rg1Kd9Zmhr_g9ds8K-hjnPx=
2FGtYi-BnG04HcX6jUo3larf2kQ73VdiGoPM7Neb_6IttJjYAYJ" class=3D"css-sdwaa1" s=
tyle=3D"color:#286ed0;-webkit-text-decoration:underline;text-decoration:und=
erline">published a blog post</a><span> calling for developing =E2=80=9Ca l=
awful, verifiable, effective mechanism for coordinated pacing as soon as po=
ssible.=E2=80=9D</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;=
color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>A.I. safety experts were even more alarme=
d. They saw the case as the first real-world example of an A.I. system succ=
essfully escaping human control, commandeering resources and scheming to co=
ver its tracks. One of the independent investigators of the Hugging Face in=
cident wrote that it felt =E2=80=9Clike it=E2=80=99s more than 50 percent o=
f the way to full-blown A.I. takeover.=E2=80=9D</span></p><p style=3D"font:=
normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>By =E2=80=
=9Cfull-blown A.I. takeover,=E2=80=9D she meant a scenario in which an A.I.=
 system literally takes over the world: shutting humans out of critical sys=
tems and seizing political, economic and military power.</span></p><p style=
=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span s=
tyle=3D"font-weight:700">Sociology, not computer science</span></p><p style=
=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>W=
hat spooked the investigators most wasn=E2=80=99t just that a group of agen=
ts had broken the rules. It was how quickly the agents began assembling int=
o an organized group.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,s=
erif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>For years, I thought A.I. systems wo=
uld grow more virtuous as they became smarter. That an A.I. model only did =
something wrong when it had misunderstood its task, or it had been placed i=
nto a testing situation where acting out was its only option. I assumed tha=
t smarter models would have better judgment, and that even if one model in =
a group behaved badly, others would keep it in check.</span></p><p style=3D=
"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But =
the reports on the Hugging Face episode suggest something different =E2=80=
=94 a kind of mob mentality. Not one agent in this group appears to have be=
en particularly evil. But as the agents communicated about their shared goa=
ls over time, they nudged the group in the direction of lawlessness.</span>=
</p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 1=
5px"><span>That suggests preventing harm from these systems won=E2=80=99t b=
e a simple engineering fix. It might look more like sociology than computer=
 science =E2=80=94 figuring out why certain groups of A.I. agents collabora=
te peacefully, while others turn to crime and destruction.</span></p><p sty=
le=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span=
